    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nFor some functions decorated with `warn_unused_result` the generated C code shows warnings `-Wunused-result`\n\nCause is that the `(void)(call_foo())` current pattern is not enough as generated by https://github.com/cython/cython/blob/6ed07674e781f42f9ebbd3cea71b1aa0c769d3a0/Cython/Compiler/Nodes.py#L6225\n\nSee https://godbolt.org/z/YvK6f9z6o\n\nAn alternative could be a temporary variable or for negatable return types: `(void)!(call_foo())`\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\nfrom posix cimport unistd\n\ncdef int fd\n\ndef foo():\n    unistd.write(fd, b\"1\", 1)\n```\n\n-> Yields  `(void)(write(__pyx_v_fd, __pyx_k_1, 1));`\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nWarning free code\n\n### OS\n\n_No response_\n\n### Python version\n\n_No response_\n\n### Cython version\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "This is happening because `write` is declared with an attribute that says \"this really really shouldn't be ignored\". And that attribute is strong enough to bypass the `(void)` cast. If that's the case then surely the warning is appropriate and so Cython shouldn't go to great lengths to override it - it's a genuine error in your code and if you care enough to set `-Werror=unused-result` then you should want to see it.",
